1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,070
Chai Corner now has five
outlets and a small head office.

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:07,416
But on winter evenings,
sales drop at all five.

3
00:00:07,966 --> 00:00:10,039
Kabir says, sell hot snacks.

4
00:00:10,039 --> 00:00:12,061
Asha says, stay open later.

5
00:00:12,361 --> 00:00:14,739
And Meena wants a new ginger flavour.

6
00:00:15,039 --> 00:00:18,323
Three ideas, and nobody
knows which one to pick.

7
00:00:18,973 --> 00:00:20,456
Ravi stops guessing.

8
00:00:20,456 --> 00:00:22,053
He follows a process.

9
00:00:22,753 --> 00:00:25,303
Ravi now works from a small head office.

10
00:00:25,653 --> 00:00:28,354
Five pins on his map, one for each outlet.

11
00:00:28,704 --> 00:00:32,424
Every week, the outlets send him
questions, and he must choose.

12
00:00:32,774 --> 00:00:36,324
And one wrong choice now hits
all five outlets at once.

13
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:38,806
So, what is decision making?

14
00:00:39,106 --> 00:00:44,123
Decision making means choosing the best
option from two or more alternatives.

15
00:00:44,623 --> 00:00:48,213
Alternatives just means the
different options you could pick.

16
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:51,783
If there is only one way,
there is nothing to decide.

17
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:56,812
And managers decide all day: what to
buy, who to hire, and when to open.

18
00:00:57,462 --> 00:00:59,784
Good decisions follow seven steps.

19
00:00:59,784 --> 00:01:01,321
Watch Ravi use them.

20
00:01:01,771 --> 00:01:02,671
One.

21
00:01:02,301 --> 00:01:03,837
Identify the problem.

22
00:01:03,837 --> 00:01:07,471
Evening sales are down by
a third, at every outlet.

23
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:08,720
Two.

24
00:01:08,347 --> 00:01:09,709
Gather information.

25
00:01:09,709 --> 00:01:13,580
Kabir checks the sales, and
the team asks customers why.

26
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:14,830
Three.

27
00:01:14,541 --> 00:01:19,059
List the alternatives: hot snacks,
longer hours, or a new flavour.

28
00:01:19,409 --> 00:01:20,309
Four.

29
00:01:19,963 --> 00:01:21,281
Evaluate each one.

30
00:01:21,281 --> 00:01:23,014
Snacks are cheap to add.

31
00:01:23,014 --> 00:01:25,658
Longer hours mean paying staff at night.

32
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:28,214
A new flavour takes time to catch on.

33
00:01:28,564 --> 00:01:29,464
Five.

34
00:01:29,123 --> 00:01:30,351
Choose the best.

35
00:01:30,351 --> 00:01:33,177
Hot snacks cost little and bring the most.

36
00:01:33,527 --> 00:01:34,427
Six.

37
00:01:34,086 --> 00:01:34,986
Implement.

38
00:01:34,974 --> 00:01:38,426
Hot snacks go on the
menu at all five outlets.

39
00:01:38,776 --> 00:01:39,676
And seven.

40
00:01:39,629 --> 00:01:40,529
Follow up.

41
00:01:40,482 --> 00:01:42,894
After one month, check the results.

42
00:01:43,544 --> 00:01:46,039
One month later, Kabir brings the numbers.

43
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:49,037
Evening sales are back up at four outlets.

44
00:01:49,387 --> 00:01:53,052
But at the college outlet, students
say they want it open later.

45
00:01:53,402 --> 00:01:56,666
So Ravi runs the steps
again, for that one outlet.

46
00:01:56,666 --> 00:01:58,852
Follow up feeds the next decision.

47
00:01:59,502 --> 00:02:03,172
The thinker Herbert Simon grouped
these steps into three phases.

48
00:02:03,522 --> 00:02:06,399
Intelligence, which here
means finding things out.

49
00:02:06,399 --> 00:02:08,659
Spot the problem, and get the facts.

50
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:09,909
Design.

51
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:12,528
Find the options, and
think each one through.

52
00:02:12,878 --> 00:02:13,778
Choice.

53
00:02:13,518 --> 00:02:14,418
Pick one.

54
00:02:14,314 --> 00:02:16,518
Then act on it, and review it.

55
00:02:17,168 --> 00:02:19,833
So what do these steps
look like at work today?

56
00:02:20,183 --> 00:02:21,555
Define the problem.

57
00:02:21,555 --> 00:02:25,126
A hospital first measures how
long patients really wait.

58
00:02:25,476 --> 00:02:26,650
Gather facts.

59
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:32,206
An online shop reads reviews and return
reasons before it changes a product.

60
00:02:32,556 --> 00:02:33,906
Weigh the options.

61
00:02:33,906 --> 00:02:37,296
A school compares bus
companies on cost and safety.

62
00:02:37,646 --> 00:02:38,966
Implement with care.

63
00:02:38,966 --> 00:02:42,964
A bank trains staff at every branch
before a new system goes live.

64
00:02:43,314 --> 00:02:44,214
Follow up.

65
00:02:44,185 --> 00:02:48,002
A factory checks its error
rate every week after a change.

66
00:02:48,652 --> 00:02:51,351
But is following every step always wise?

67
00:02:51,351 --> 00:02:53,798
The honest answer is, yes and no.

68
00:02:54,148 --> 00:02:55,301
The good side.

69
00:02:55,301 --> 00:02:59,486
Fewer blind guesses, clear
reasons, and everyone can see why.

70
00:02:59,836 --> 00:03:00,926
The hard side.

71
00:03:00,926 --> 00:03:04,049
The steps take time, and
good facts cost money.

72
00:03:04,399 --> 00:03:05,970
And people bring bias.

73
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:09,635
Bias means leaning one way
before you look at the facts.

74
00:03:09,985 --> 00:03:13,105
Also, not every choice
deserves seven steps.

75
00:03:13,105 --> 00:03:15,909
Nobody needs a meeting to reorder sugar.

76
00:03:16,259 --> 00:03:18,026
So the lesson is balance.

77
00:03:18,026 --> 00:03:20,246
Big choices get the full process.

78
00:03:20,246 --> 00:03:22,220
Small ones get a quick rule.

79
00:03:22,870 --> 00:03:23,770
Quick check.

80
00:03:23,757 --> 00:03:26,344
Hot snacks are now on the menu everywhere.

81
00:03:26,344 --> 00:03:27,852
What is the next step?

82
00:03:29,752 --> 00:03:30,652
Follow up!

83
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:33,939
Check the results, to see if
the decision really worked.

84
00:03:34,539 --> 00:03:35,549
So, remember.

85
00:03:35,849 --> 00:03:40,001
Decision making means choosing the
best option among alternatives.

86
00:03:40,351 --> 00:03:44,757
Define the problem, gather facts, list
and weigh the options, then choose.

87
00:03:45,107 --> 00:03:47,364
Then implement, and always follow up.

88
00:03:47,964 --> 00:03:52,361
Next time, we'll see why some decisions
are routine, and others are brand

89
00:03:52,361 --> 00:03:53,261
new.
